# Weather, environmental readings and warnings

Say what was measured or forecast, where it applies and when the source supplied it. Keep forecast probability, measured rainfall and warning severity apart. A theme must not turn an ordinary record into an emergency signal. Missing data never means a safe condition.

<h2 id="scope-and-native-ownership">What Home Assistant owns</h2>

A weather entity gives current conditions as state attributes, such as temperature, humidity, pressure, wind and the units for each, depending on the provider. Forecasts come from the `weather.get_forecasts` action. Warning feeds can come from separate integrations. The provider decides the units, the observation, issue and valid times, the area and the warning wording. A shared card cannot invent them. See the [Home Assistant weather documentation](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/weather/), which opens outside this site.

This is reference specification HA-GAP-027. The application must also meet the integration acceptance checks below. The examples are simulated and do not show that a feature or adapter is installed.

<h2 id="states-and-content-decisions">What to show for each condition</h2>

| Condition | Show | Do not |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Observed reading | The quantity, unit, place and when it was observed. | Present the time of the last state change as a new measurement. |
| Forecast period | The period covered and the forecast quantity. | Present a forecast as a current reading. |
| Warning request succeeded, none found | No matching warnings for the area and time checked. | Say the household is safe or that every region is clear. |
| Warning data unavailable | That the feed is missing or failed, kept apart from an empty result. | Show a green all-clear. |
| Warning active or expired | The source's wording, the area it applies to and the valid period. | Quietly extend an expired warning. |

<h2 id="integration-contract">Rules for the integration</h2>

1. Keep the provider's credit and official warning text where they are supplied. Use the source's severity definitions and a separate meaning-based colour role. The chosen brand palette never sets severity.
2. Keep the issue time, observation time, retrieval time and valid period apart. If a time is not supplied, say so. Do not take it from a different timestamp.
3. Use the units Home Assistant is configured with and the provider's meaning for each quantity. Label rain probability as a percentage. Label accumulated rain with its unit and whether it was measured or forecast, and the period it covers.
4. Apply the configured place or area exactly. A nearby warning may need an explanation, but do not say it matches the household unless the integration shows that it does.

<h2 id="accessible-interaction">Keyboard and screen reader use</h2>

- Write the condition as text beside any weather symbol, and tell warnings apart without colour. Keep all essential warning content available without a tooltip.
- Show forecasts in a table with clear periods and units. Keep missing entries as missing instead of filling the gap with zero.
- Respect reduced motion. Animation and photographic backgrounds must not make values, the source credit or actions harder to read.

<h2 id="failure-and-recovery">When something goes wrong</h2>

- If a feed fails, say when information was last received and that the current status is unknown. Do not reuse an earlier empty result as if it were current.
- If only part of a forecast is available, say the coverage is incomplete and keep real zeros apart from missing values.
- For safety information, keep the route to the official source and its area. Do not suggest that Home Assistant replaces an alarm, a physical check or official emergency guidance.

